# ================= E-12 =================
E12 = '''A yes covers ONE take. A retake (step 20) needs a new take id and a new yes. In the sentence above this one, "the lead" means the assistant and "window end" means the end of the spending window.
Before you say yes, the assistant sends you one message, called the request for your yes. It has two parts. If any part is missing, you answer no.
Part 1, the money figures: the figures that the sentence above lists (at most 3 model calls, the worst case per call, 3 times that worst case, the money spent so far, the cap and the end of the spending window as the settings show them, and the planned end of the spending window). The dollar figure "3 times that worst case" is the `<X>` of your reply.
Part 2 is this list:
- Four facts. The assistant gives each file with exactly one of these four labels: "result file", "reviewers' report", "search file" and "test output file".
  1. The stand-in test and the flow rehearsal have been run, and how their checks ended, in numbers. A check is one numbered test on the plan's list of checks. The form is: "stand-in test: N1 pass, 0 failed, 0 missed, 0 not run; flow rehearsal: N2 pass, 0 failed, 0 missed, 0 not run; crash check: tested or untested". N1 is the number of checks of the stand-in test that ended "pass", and N2 is the same for the flow rehearsal. Each must equal the number of checks on that test's list, not counting the crash check. The crash check (see below) has its own slot and is never counted in N1, N2, "failed", "missed" or "not run". "Failed" means the check ran and showed a bad result. "Missed" means the check ran but could not see what it was looking for. "Not run" means the check was never carried out.
  2. The "result file": the file where the written result of both tests is saved.
  3. Reviewers who are not the assistant (separate review programs, named in their own report) have read that result. The file is the "reviewers' report".
  4. The last step of the take program sends no delete request to HubSpot (a delete request is a message that asks HubSpot to delete a contact). The assistant names its evidence in one of two ways. Either the "search file" (a saved search of the final code that finds no delete request) together with the "test output file" (a saved test in which a fake HubSpot records every call of that step and none is a delete request); or the name of the commit (one saved version of the code) that removes the delete request, written in the slot of the "search file", together with the "test output file" that shows it. The reviewers' report must have read this evidence.
- The list of checks of the plan, attached to the request.
- The days and times, in UTC and IST. The take is planned for Wed 18 Nov 2026. The request states the planned start time and the latest start time. The assistant asks for no start later than 18:04 UTC = 23:34 IST on any day, because the take needs about 14 minutes. The take program itself accepts a start until about 18:14 UTC = 23:44 IST; that figure is a reading of step 17 and is not checked in the code.
- The last day. Mon 23 Nov 2026 is the LAST day on which a take may run. On that day the spending window ends at 18:29 UTC = 23:59 IST (after that the model stage may not spend money; the older text calls it "the window end"). The last time to revoke the secret key for the model is 18:19 UTC = 23:49 IST that day (step 22).
- No take is planned for Mon 23 Nov. The assistant asks for none unless it has first shown you, in writing, the studio lead's reading of the final take program on three points:
  1. When can the program first spend money?
  2. Does it refuse to start after the end of the spending window?
  3. What does it do, and which number does it return when it stops (an exit code: 0 means it ended normally, any other number means it ended with a problem), when its secret key is revoked during a run?
  If it then asks for a take on Mon 23 Nov, it asks for no start later than 18:04 UTC = 23:34 IST, so that the take is over before 18:19 UTC = 23:49 IST.
- If a take is still running at 18:19 UTC = 23:49 IST, the assistant tells you in writing what happens next (step 22 says what you do with the key).
"Passed" means both of these: every check ended "pass", and the reviewers left no finding open (a finding is one problem that a reviewer wrote down in the reviewers' report). A check that ended "failed", "missed" or "not run" is not a pass. Then you do not say yes and the take does not go ahead; the assistant tells you what happens next. There is ONE exception, the crash check: the check of a crash between sending the mail and recording in the take's database that it was sent. The request gives one of four words for it. "Tested": it ran and ended "pass". "Untested": the plan runs it a second time if the first run ended "missed", and both runs ended "missed"; the request must then say "crash check untested" in these words, the take may go ahead on your yes, and you must not post that the take is crash-safe. "Failed": it ran and showed a double mail or a lost enquiry. "Not run": it was never carried out. For "failed" and "not run" you answer no. (Crash-safe means: the take survives a crash at any moment without a double mail or a lost enquiry.)
Your reply is ONE line that you type in the chat. Copy this form, replace every part in angle brackets and keep the rest as written: "Yes, take <take id>; stand-in test <N1> pass, 0 failed, 0 missed, 0 not run; flow rehearsal <N2> pass, 0 failed, 0 missed, 0 not run; crash check <tested or untested>; result file <result file>; reviewers' report <reviewers' report file>; search file <search file or commit name>; test output file <test output file>; up to 3 model calls and up to US$<X>; spending window ends 2026-11-23 18:29 UTC = 23:59 IST; latest start <latest start time in UTC and IST>; I know that the assistant shares my Linux user, my clipboard and my screen."
- `<take id>` is the short name you choose for this take, new every take. Use only lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, start with a letter, keep it to at most 20 characters and use no spaces; it becomes part of the take's mail addresses (`take-<id>-a@example.com`). This rule is the assistant's safe choice and is not read from the program (UNVERIFIED): if the program refuses the id, you choose another one. Choose it before you reply, and use the same id in your reply and in step 17. `<N1>` is the number that the request gives for the checks of the stand-in test that ended "pass", and `<N2>` the same for the flow rehearsal; type exactly those two numbers. `<tested or untested>`: write `tested`, or `untested` only if the request says "crash check untested". The four names after "result file", "reviewers' report", "search file" and "test output file" are copied exactly from the request, one name into each slot with the same label. `<X>` is the dollar figure that the request gives as 3 times the worst case per call. `<latest start time in UTC and IST>` is copied from the request.
- The last part, "I know that the assistant shares my Linux user, my clipboard and my screen.", is REQUIRED. Without it the assistant does not start the take. Your Linux user is your sign-in to the Linux part of your laptop (WSL); the programs that the assistant starts there run under it, so they can read every file that you can read.
You are the one who answers no: answer no if the request leaves out a fact, a file name or the list of checks, or says that anything did not pass. You may send other messages before your reply (a question, a request to show a result file, or the request about filming of step 19); only your reply in the form above can start a take. If a part of your reply is missing or wrong, the assistant does not start the take and tells you which part. The assistant does not start the take on anything but your written reply in this form. You may ask the assistant to show a named result file or report file in the chat; it never shows a secret file (`n8n.env`, a key file, a password file).'''
