# ================= E-16 =================
E16 = '''deletes all saved runs of the take (made-up data only, owner decision 2026-11-16), turns off the setting that saves successful runs (a setting of the take's own n8n, not of each workflow), stops n8n, removes the take folder and the container of the database server. The assistant does all of this; there is nothing for you to do. It reports each part as done or not done. If a part is not done, it tells you before you start your own steps (items 2 to 6).
Words used here. The database server is the Postgres program that holds n8n's database and the take's database. The container is the closed package, run by Docker, in which that program runs. Removing the container removes the program and normally the databases inside it. A Docker volume is a storage area that Docker keeps apart from a container. If the database files sit in a volume, removing the container alone may not delete them, and the assistant then also removes the volume that it created for the take. Whether a volume is used is UNVERIFIED.
What the assistant does first. Before it removes anything, it copies the take's result file and its counts (names and numbers only, no secret) into a folder of its own on the Linux disk, outside the take folder. It tells you that folder's name before the clean-up. It does this because removing the container deletes n8n's database and the take's table of enquiries too.
Which container. It removes only the container (and volume) that it started for the take. It tells you the container's name, which is on the list of containers it recorded when the run began. It touches no other container.
The question. The stand-in test is planned to test only whether removing the container also removes the saved runs. Until that is known, the assistant asks you one question in the chat before it starts item 1, and only if the method is still undecided: "May the saved runs be deleted only by removing the container?" Reply with exactly `Yes, remove the container only` or `No`. If you reply `Yes, remove the container only`: the assistant removes the container and does not delete the saved runs one by one first, so nothing shows before the removal that the runs are gone. If you reply `No`, or you do not reply, the answer is No: the assistant first turns off the saving setting, deletes the saved runs in n8n and tells you that they are gone, and only then removes the container; this takes a few more minutes'''
